{"id":"m2://rec/open_logic/content/sets-functions-relations/functions/function-basics.tex#ex-4#1","source":"open_logic","snapshot":"blake3:a5eb116529d3e799289dd9f7e7530d0d446b3615f5608fe08c668241eee3c765","locator":"https://github.com/OpenLogicProject/OpenLogic/blob/1e960beff9ed7835bf3e3f1335e21af3439cd107/content/sets-functions-relations/functions/function-basics.tex#L92","native_id":"content/sets-functions-relations/functions/function-basics.tex#ex-4","kind":"example","text":"Let $f \\colon \\Nat \\to \\Nat$ be defined such that $f(x) = x+1$. This\nis a definition that specifies $f$ as a function which takes in\nnatural numbers and outputs natural numbers. It tells us that, given a\nnatural number~$x$, $f$ will output its successor~$x+1$.\nIn this case, the codomain $\\Nat$ is not the range of~$f$, since the\nnatural number~$0$ is not the successor of any natural number. The\nrange of~$f$ is the set of all positive integers, $\\Int^{+}$.","class":"open","by":"textbook/1","resource":"PKGMM3P5QY","at":{"section":"78.9","kind":"ex"}}
